    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Lothair III, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Lothair III, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothair_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> began as a frontier march on the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, in lands settled by Slavic peoples such as the Hevelli on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a>. Otto I had founded a bishopric at Brandenburg in 948, but the Great Slav Rising of 983 drove the Germans back beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, and for a century and a half the Northern March existed mostly as a title. In 1134 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Lothair III</a> gave the Northern March to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>. Albert had an understanding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribislav-henry">Pribislav-Henry</a>, the Christian prince of the Hevelli, who made him his heir, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribislav-of-mecklenburg">Pribislav</a>’s death in 1150 he took over the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a>. A Slav prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaxa-of-kopenick">Jaxa of Köpenick</a>, then seized it, but Albert recaptured it on 11 June 1157, the date traditionally taken as the founding of the margraviate, and thereafter styled himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>. His Ascanian successors pushed east through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelland">Havelland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teltow">Teltow</a> and Barnim and beyond the Oder into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a>, bringing in German and Flemish settlers, founding Cistercian monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lehnin-abbey">Lehnin</a> in 1180, and chartering towns. Among them were the twin settlements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, first recorded in 1237 and 1244, which were united in administration in 1307.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin.jpg" alt="Berlin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museumsinsel_Berlin_Juli_2021_1_(cropped)_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kasa Fue; derivative work by Georgfotoart, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mark was poor land by German standards: sandy soils, pine forest, heath, lakes and marshes, nicknamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>’s sandbox. Its economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a>, barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, fish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and its towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin-Cölln</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> an der Oder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stendal">Stendal</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a>, traded grain and cloth along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder, several joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. From the fifteenth century the nobility, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junkers">Junkers</a>, turned their estates into large grain-producing manors worked by peasants bound by heavy labour services, a system of manorial lordship, <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutsherrschaft">Gutsherrschaft</a></em>, that shaped east-Elbian society until the nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector">Joachim II</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> in 1539, taking communion in both kinds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spandau">Spandau</a>, and the elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg">John Sigismund</a> became a Calvinist in 1613, leaving a Reformed dynasty ruling a Lutheran people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> was catastrophic: armies of both sides crossed the Mark, and parts of it may have lost half their population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">The Great Elector</a> rebuilt by encouraging immigration, above all through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam">Edict of Potsdam</a> of 1685, which invited some 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> who brought new crafts and industries. Canals, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-wilhelm-canal">Friedrich-Wilhelm Canal</a> linking the Oder and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, built in the 1660s, connected the river systems and helped Berlin grow into a commercial centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fehrbellin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fehrbellin.jpg" alt="Battle of Fehrbellin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fehrbellin">Battle of Fehrbellin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Fehrbellin2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dismar Degen, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In its first centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> was a conquering frontier power, extending its rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and contending with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern</a>, Frederick I, made his authority real by breaking the castles of robber barons such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quitzow-family">Quitzow family</a> in 1414 with heavy artillery. Yet for much of the sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries the electorate had little military weight, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years’ War</a> the elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-william-elector-of-brandenburg">George William</a> could not keep either the Swedish or the imperial armies out of his lands. The turning point was the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a>, from 1640 to 1688. He built a permanent standing army that grew to some 30,000 men, fought on both sides in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, and on 28 June 1675 defeated an invading Swedish army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fehrbellin">Battle of Fehrbellin</a>, the first major victory of Brandenburg’s own forces. He also created a small navy and founded the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-friedrichsburg">Gross Friedrichsburg</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1683. After 1701 the army that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> had built became the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, with which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> conquered Silesia from 1740 and survived the Seven Years’ War of 1756–1763.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of 1356" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldene_Bulle_Nahaufnahme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karl IV., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The margrave held his land as an imperial fief, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> confirmed him as one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> who chose the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, with the office of arch-chamberlain of the Empire. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">Ascanian</a> line died out in 1320 the Mark passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> in 1323 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">Luxembourgs</a> in 1373, and suffered from weak, absent rulers. In 1415 Emperor Sigismund granted it to his supporter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick VI, Burgrave of Nuremberg</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>, who became Elector Frederick I. His successors subdued the towns, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II Irontooth</a> breaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>’s independence in 1448, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-achilles-elector-of-brandenburg">Albert Achilles</a> laid down in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dispositio-achillea">Dispositio Achillea</a></em> of 1473 that the Mark itself should pass undivided to the eldest son. The estates of nobles and towns controlled taxation, but in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-recess-of-1653">Recess of 1653</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">the Great Elector</a> confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junkers">Junkers</a>’ power over their peasants in return for taxes to support his army. He then built a central administration under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-war-commissariat">General War Commissariat</a> that collected taxes across his scattered lands, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> and Mark in the west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> in the east. This combination of a bureaucracy funding an army, a noble officer class and a Reformed dynasty ruling a largely Lutheran people became the foundation of the Prussian state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kleve"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kleve.jpg" alt="Kleve" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Kleve</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleve_Schwanenturm_vom_Kleiner_Markt_PM17-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pieter Delicaat, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> was the nucleus of the Prussian monarchy and therefore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> founded in 1871. The inheritances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Mark and Ravensberg in 1614 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> in 1618, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/further-pomerania">Farther Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> and the expectancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, so that a minor electorate became the core of a composite state stretching across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1701 Elector Frederick III took the title King Frederick I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, the electors’ residence since the late fifteenth century, became a royal capital and later the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Brandenburg’s welcome to religious refugees, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> to Bohemian Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expulsion-of-the-salzburg-protestants">Salzburg Lutherans</a>, became a lasting element of Prussian state policy and enriched Berlin’s economy and culture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-frankfurt-oder">University of Frankfurt an der Oder</a>, the Viadrina, founded in 1506, was the Mark’s first university. The German colonisation of the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">Ascanian</a> margraves took a leading part, reshaped the ethnic and linguistic map of central Europe, though the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> survive as a minority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junkers">Junker</a> estate economy and the close bond between monarchy, nobility and army that grew up in Brandenburg shaped Prussian and German politics into the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jena-auerstedt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-jena-auerstedt.jpg" alt="Battle of Jena–Auerstedt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jena-auerstedt">Battle of Jena–Auerstedt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> did not decline so much as dissolve into the larger state it had created. After the coronation of Frederick I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> on 18 January 1701 the electorate remained a distinct territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> continued to sit in the imperial college of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> as electors of Brandenburg, but government was increasingly organised for the Prussian monarchy as a whole. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William</a> I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> the Mark was the heartland of the kingdom, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> as its seats of government and garrisons. Its separate existence ended with the Empire itself. In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jena-auerstedt">Jena and Auerstedt</a> broke the Prussian army, French troops entered Berlin, and the Holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> had already been dissolved on 6 August 1806 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> laid down the imperial crown, so that the electoral title lost its meaning. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the Prussian reforms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> reorganised the monarchy, and in 1815 the Mark became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-brandenburg">Province of Brandenburg</a>, from which Berlin was separated in 1881. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a> east of the Oder passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Prussia was abolished in 1947, and the territory west of the Oder is today the German federal state of Brandenburg, surrounding Berlin.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> turned a sandy frontier march east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> into the electoral seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a>, whose standing army, bureaucracy and inheritances created the Prussian state and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> the future capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</em></p>

